# Race Condition

A race condition occurs when the behavior of a program depends on the relative timing or interleaving of multiple threads/processes accessing shared resources, and that timing is not properly controlled. The outcome becomes non-deterministic—correct only if operations happen in a particular order, which isn't guaranteed.

## Example a Unit Test Would Miss

Consider a singleton getter:

```java
if (instance == null) {
    instance = new Instance();
}
return instance;
```

Two threads calling this simultaneously might both see `instance == null` and each create a new object, breaking the singleton guarantee.

A unit test running this method sequentially (single-threaded) will always pass—there's no concurrency to expose the flaw. Even multithreaded tests often miss it because the race window is tiny; the bug may appear only occasionally, under specific timing, thread scheduling, or hardware load (e.g., in production under high concurrency). This makes race conditions notoriously hard to catch with standard, deterministic unit tests.

